note top of AUTHORITY
  Twelve producer verdicts remain bound to frozen
  bytes, separate from corrected seal reviews.
  Four current seal verdicts accept the qualified
  generation, but emission requires a separate
  lead release that grants no publication rights.
end note

note bottom of CURRENT
  Disabling old bindings must not consume the
  inner grant or raise its original ceiling.
  Keep the two approval identities distinct so
  a source rebind cannot renew the budget or
  redirect the original issuer.
end note

note right of PHASE_A
  Phase A must reject extra fault failure codes
  and unrelated clock, deadline or cleanup errors.
  Authority reviews qualify only those records;
  the twelve source verdicts accept frozen bytes,
  not operational readiness or publication rights.
end note

note left of SNAPSHOT
  The freeze pins copied spec inputs while the
  current diagrams continue to track new phases.
  Later checks must use those frozen copies for
  spec rows, not replay a historical writer
  against the mutable originals.
end note

note right of SOURCE
  Source proofs exclude binding and ready hashes,
  so qualification can finish before they exist.
  Runtime identity adds the actual capture proof
  and binding hash, but omits readiness to prevent
  a cycle through the caller.
end note

note left of SOURCE
  Passing suites do not protect readiness when
  the validator trusts a receipt to set its count.
  The consumer must enforce the qualified suite
  count and genuine child evidence, rather than
  accept matching claims with missing witnesses.
end note

note left of IMPLEMENTATION
  Expected faults must not mask a deadline breach
  during evidence finalization.
  The collector must track a separate violation
  and reject it through the real fault predicate,
  with a combined failure exercising that path.
end note

note right of IMPLEMENTATION
  Persistent final clock failure must not prevent
  an open journal from receiving a close attempt.
  Cleanup must preserve sticky clock and close
  errors without inventing timestamps, clock
  recovery, or a successful capture result.
end note

note left of QUALIFICATION
  Check fixed helper pins before qualification
  can import or execute those helper bytes.
  Expected digests belong in qualified source,
  so modified helpers cannot provide the identity
  used to validate their own contents.
end note

note right of QUALIFICATION
  Runtime must compare declared helper metadata,
  without reading or resolving the test helper.
  Qualified source and proof evidence bind its
  fixed pin, preserving the production read
  closure without adding packet inputs.
end note

note left of DELIVERY
  Check the whole literal table before writing
  any member into the snapshot tree.
  Then import only verified snapshot modules and
  use a bounded reader, so unchecked inputs cannot
  bypass source, proof, or readiness validation.
end note

note right of DELIVERY
  Batching changes review timing, not the gates
  that qualify a completed source generation.
  The completed four-role batch accepts these
  frozen bytes, so later source edits require new
  qualification rather than borrowed verdicts.
end note

note bottom of DELIVERY
  A passing summary cannot qualify a command
  whose observed streams contain no test output.
  The consumer must bind summary text to actual
  stream sizes and digests, so a no-op child
  cannot borrow a successful suite declaration.
end note

note right of OUTPUT_GOAL
  Output failures must enter a sticky latch
  that reuses the existing owned cleanup path.
  Fold known errors before outcome finalization,
  so asynchronous writes cannot bypass cleanup
  or publish success before resource closure.
end note

note bottom of OUTPUT_GOAL
  Keep the failure file when owned cleanup is
  unconfirmed, but expose no public outcome path.
  Late write errors must invalidate the candidate
  through external nonzero exit, not a rewritten
  outcome or a second terminal message.
end note

note right of RELEASE3
  Release binds qualified source and runtime pins,
  not its own hash or the table that includes it.
  Later packet and publication hashes stay outside
  this record, so sealing can extend the chain
  without feeding its results back into release.
end note

note right of EPIPE
  Caller and supervisor exits describe distinct
  actors, so one status cannot stand in for both.
  Keep direct child facts separate from the outer
  exit observation, because overwriting a caller
  result cannot establish that cleanup failed.
end note

note right of DERIVATION
  The existing eligibility check also reads ready,
  so binding alone cannot close the chain.
  Hash binding bytes before runtime and readiness,
  then validate both through finite packet reads
  without changing the frozen producer schema.
end note

note right of CLOSURE
  Observed reads cannot prove the full closure,
  because untested paths may import other modules.
  Keep static reads, imports and required packet
  members distinct, so documentary pins never form
  a recursive production resolver.
end note

note right of OBSERVATION_CORRECTION
  A static list cannot establish observed reads,
  so the companion withdraws both pinned claims.
  Qualification and emission require this record,
  preserving the seven original frozen records
  without treating unknown reads as an empty set.
end note

note right of PROOF_GOAL
  A proof must bind its qualification manifest,
  not merely accept the presence of field names.
  Check dependencies and derived records against
  preserved inputs from the same generation, so
  PASS text cannot certify unrelated fit claims.
end note

note right of GENERATION_GOAL
  Fresh disk hashes cannot identify cached code
  or stop a later audit from mixing generations.
  Bind executing functions and post-gate reads to
  the qualified source generation, so emission and
  audit cannot silently switch to different bytes.
end note

note right of TEST_GOAL
  Malformed fixtures reject before reader runs,
  so their failure cannot exercise deeper gates.
  Start each negative from a complete accepted
  candidate, then assert the named downstream
  guard rather than an unrelated schema rejection.
end note

note right of POSTCHECK
  Fresh metadata may describe changed inputs,
  not the expectation bound to a prior emission.
  Keep the emitted expectation stable, so a fresh
  postcheck rejects input mutations instead of
  normalizing drift into a new baseline.
end note

note right of EMISSION_GATE
  An in-memory candidate proves no emitted bytes,
  and syntax checks cannot authorize publication.
  Keep emission behind its separate lead gate, so
  fresh-process postchecks bind the output bytes
  without fabricating future result or audit pins.
end note

note left of PUBLICATION
  Sealed release and packet publication must
  precede production rendering with actual pins.
  Command publication follows rendering, so its
  absence must block the operator run rather than
  create a cycle in the render gate.
end note

note right of RENDER
  Fixed receipts must bind bytes to observed
  commands and Git objects, not create custody.
  Unsigned records cannot prove approval or remote
  events alone, so byte consistency must not be
  claimed as an independent grant.
end note

note bottom of RENDER
  Disk hashes describe the files now on disk,
  not the cached functions already executing.
  Match renderer and serialized bootstrap
  fingerprints to the qualified release, so older
  imports cannot borrow evidence from newer files.
end note

note right of HANDOFF
  Rendering serializes only the runtime function,
  so an external helper would be unavailable.
  Controlled progress stays inside that function
  and reuses its clocks, requests, and test writer
  to preserve the existing cleanup contract.
end note

